USB gadget driver for the Google Pixel 7

The driver 'exynos_usb_gadget' makes the phone a USB device. It is a port
of Linux' DWC3 controller driver in device mode with the UDC core and the
composite framework, the vendor's USB PHY driver (with the USBDP combo PHY
for SuperSpeed), and a slim glue with the vendor's register setup of the
controller. Its clients are the functions of the device: each one has a
Usb_gadget session (include/usb_gadget_session), describes itself with
descriptors of function-local numbers (include/usb_gadget/descriptor.h),
and moves data with packets. The driver registers the device once every
function listed in its config is described:

! + config | vendor_id: 0x1d6b | product_id: 0x0104 | manufacturer: Genode
!          | product: Pixel 7 | serial: 0001 | max_power_ma: 100
!   + function | label: echo

A function is matched by the last element of its session label. Before
the kernel starts, the driver closes the USB switch of the MAX77759's
Type-C controller and reads the cable orientation via an I2c session
(bus address 0x25, writes of 2 bytes). The orientation is read once, a
cable plugged in or flipped later keeps the SuperSpeed lanes of the start.
The isolation of the PHYs, which only the secure monitor controls, is
released by bootstrap.
It needs the ports 'pixel_linux' and 'google_soc_gs'.

The logic of include/usb_gadget has host tests:

! make -C repos/pixel/src/test/usb_gadget_host

The scenario 'run/usb_gadget_pixel' runs the function
'test-usb_gadget_echo', a serial port that echoes every byte. With the
phone booted, the PC checks the echo via the port's USB IDs:

! make run/usb_gadget_pixel KERNEL=hw BOARD=google_pixel7
! repos/pixel/tool/usb_echo_check
